# =============================================================================
# .env.example — committed defaults. .env (local, git-ignored) overrides them,
# the shell environment overrides .env, and a make command-line VAR=value
# overrides everything: `APT_SNAPSHOT_DATE=… make bake-prod` means that date.
# A value here is LITERAL text: no ${VAR} expansion and no $(command) — pass
# those on the make command line instead.
# LANG/TZ/DEBIAN_FRONTEND are the exception — the files win over the shell, so
# a host locale never reaches the image. Quotes around a value are dropped, an
# unquoted ` # …` tail is a comment. `make setup` copies this file to .env.

# [Usage Instructions]
#   1. `make setup` or `cp .env.example .env`
#   2. Edit .env and change COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME to isolate your project.
#   3. .env is git-ignored for security and configuration isolation.
#   Of the ~100 keys below, a new project has to decide five: COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME,
#   ENV, ROS_DISTRO, UV_EXTRA and ROS_DOMAIN_ID. Everything else has a working
#   default — docs/GETTING_STARTED.md lists the five with what they change.
#
# [Security Notice]
#   This file may contain sensitive values (API keys, tokens, etc.).
#   NOTE: the build-time keys below (UV_SYNC_FLAGS, CMAKE_EXTRA_ARGS,
#   COLCON_EXTRA_FLAGS, UV_EXTRA, CUDA_VERSION, the snapshot dates, LANG/TZ …)
#   are passed to `docker build` as --build-arg and are therefore visible in
#   `docker history` and inside every baked artifact. A credential must NOT go
#   in them: use a BuildKit secret mount, or fetch it at runtime.
#   NEVER commit .env to version control. Use Docker Secrets or a vault
#   for production secret management. See: https://docs.docker.com/compose/how-tos/use-secrets/
# =============================================================================

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 1. CORE PROJECT SETTINGS
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

#   COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME: Unique identifier for Docker resources (Required to change)
#   Changing this value completely isolates the Docker environment for a new project.
#   Allowed: lowercase letters, digits, dashes (-), and underscores (_).
#   Must start and end with a lowercase letter or digit.
COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=myproject

#   ENV: which stack `make` acts on — 'ros' (default) or 'dev' (no ROS).
#   A dev-only fork sets it here instead of typing ENV=dev every time.
# ENV=ros

#   CONTAINER_USER: Username for the non-root developer user inside the container.
#   Files created inside the container will be owned by this user.
#   For host/container file compatibility, the user's UID and GID are dynamically
#   detected from the host by scripts/check_env.sh at build time.
#   You can manually override them by exporting HOST_UID and HOST_GID in your shell if needed.
CONTAINER_USER=user

#   HOST_WORKSPACE_PATH: Absolute path to the project on your HOST machine.
#   Used for Docker volume mounting. (Default: current folder)
# HOST_WORKSPACE_PATH=/home/user/DevKit

#   WORKSPACE_PATH: Absolute path to the project INSIDE the container.
#   SSOT for all internal tools and scripts. (Default: /workspace)
# WORKSPACE_PATH=/workspace

# [ROS & OS Version]
#   BASE_IMAGE follows ROS_DISTRO unless explicitly overridden, for example
#   with a digest pin or an NVIDIA L4T base for Jetson.
#   [Option A] ROS 2 (Default)
ROS_DISTRO=humble
# BASE_IMAGE=ubuntu:22.04

#   [Option B] ROS 1 (Legacy — noetic reached end of life in May 2025)
# ROS_DISTRO=noetic
#
#   Each distro is bound to one Ubuntu release, and BASE_IMAGE is derived from
#   ROS_DISTRO unless you set it: 20.04 → noetic, foxy (both EOL) | 22.04 → humble, iron (EOL)
#   | 24.04 → jazzy, kilted, rolling. An unlisted distro is refused rather than
#   built on the wrong base. For an EOL distro pin the ROS layer to its final
#   snapshot, since the rolling mirror drops it:
# ROS_DISTRO=foxy
# ROS_SNAPSHOT_DATE=final

#   For reproducible builds, pin the base image by digest:
# BASE_IMAGE=ubuntu:22.04@sha256:<digest>
#   Get current digest: see docs/DEPLOY.md (재현성) — one recipe, one place.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 2. HARDWARE & DISPLAY (GPU Acceleration)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

#   TARGETARCH: Docker image architecture (Default: host architecture auto-detected)
# TARGETARCH=amd64

# GPU_MODE: Hybrid GPU Acceleration Mode
#   auto  : Auto detection (Default). Probe order: nvidia → tegra → ROCm →
#           Intel DRI → AMD DRI → WSL2 D3D12 → any render node → cpu.
#   nvidia: Force NVIDIA GPU (requires nvidia-container-toolkit)
#   intel : Force Intel GPU acceleration (For Intel iGPUs)
#   amd   : Force AMD GPU acceleration
#   igpu  : Vendor-neutral /dev/dri passthrough — also the SoC GPU fallback
#           (Mali, Adreno, VideoCore: platform devices with no PCI vendor id)
#   cpu   : Force Software Rendering (always works, for low-end devices/debugging)
#   'tegra' exists too, but only INSIDE the container (Jetson has no
#   /dev/nvidiactl): select it with `gpu tegra`, or let `auto` find it.
GPU_MODE=auto

#   INSTALL_INTEL_GPU_TOOLS: Include Intel iGPU diagnostics/OpenCL packages in
#   direct Docker/SIF builds. Compose iGPU profiles enable this automatically.
# INSTALL_INTEL_GPU_TOOLS=false

# [CUDA/OpenCV Build Settings]
#   OPENCV_CUDA: CMake flags emitted by `gpu opencv_args` for projects that build
#   OpenCV (or OpenCV-dependent code) from source.
#     auto : Enable CUDA when nvcc is present. It describes the TARGET, so a
#            GPU-less builder still produces a CUDA-enabled image.
#     off  : Always emit -DWITH_CUDA=OFF.
OPENCV_CUDA=auto

# [CUDA Toolkit Image Settings] - CUDA_VERSION alone decides: it describes the TARGET,
#   so a GPU-less build host still produces a CUDA image. Unset = no CUDA layer.
#   FULL_CUDA: Install complete CUDA toolkit in development images (1/true) or minimal dev toolkit/libs (false)
#   PROD_FULL_CUDA: Install the complete CUDA toolkit in baked SIFs (dev and prod; 1/true). Default off for smaller artifacts.
#   CUDA_VERSION: Target CUDA version (e.g., 11.8.0, 12.1.1, 12.8.0)
#   CUDNN_VERSION: Target cuDNN major version (e.g., 8, 9)
# FULL_CUDA=false
# PROD_FULL_CUDA=false
# CUDA_VERSION=12.8.0
# CUDNN_VERSION=9

# [Advanced GPU Tuning] - Used for multi-GPU setups or specific acceleration features
#   NVIDIA_VISIBLE_DEVICES: GPU numbers to use or 'all' (Default: all)
#   NVIDIA_GPU_COUNT: Number of GPUs to allocate (Default: all)
#   NVIDIA_DRIVER_CAPABILITIES: Scope of acceleration features (Default: all)
# NVIDIA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=all
# NVIDIA_GPU_COUNT=all
# NVIDIA_DRIVER_CAPABILITIES=all

#   Display number for GUI integration (Default: :0) and display type
#   Automatically inherited from the host environment, uncomment below if manual setting is required.
# DISPLAY=:0
# DISPLAY_TYPE=X11

# [Advanced Display Settings] - Auto-detected by Makefile but can be overridden manually
# HOST_XAUTHORITY=/home/me/.Xauthority       # Must be an existing file when set
# HOST_WAYLAND_DISPLAY=wayland-0
# HOST_XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/run/user/1000       # Must be an existing directory when set
# HOST_X11_DIR=/tmp/.X11-unix               # Must be an existing directory when set
# HOST_SSH_AUTH_SOCK=/run/user/1000/keyring/ssh  # Must be an existing UNIX socket when set

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 3. ROS NETWORKING & COMMUNICATION
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# [Docker Infrastructure]
#   PRIVILEGED: keep false — DRI/DXG/GPU devices are already granted explicitly
#   by compose. Set true only for raw device work the explicit grants miss
#   (SocketCAN setup, USB hot-plug, realtime scheduling), and prefer adding a
#   targeted `devices:`/`device_cgroup_rules:` entry instead.
#   NETWORK_MODE: 'host' (default) is what ROS 2 discovery expects — the
#   container shares the host's interfaces and loopback, so DDS multicast and
#   every node port work without mapping. It also means every port a node binds
#   is reachable on all host interfaces. 'bridge' isolates the network; then set
#   ROS_MASTER_URI/peers yourself (docs/DEVELOPMENT.md).
NETWORK_MODE=host
#   IPC_MODE: 'host' (default) shares /dev/shm and SysV IPC with the host, which
#   is what ROS 2's shared-memory transport uses for large messages. 'private'
#   isolates it at the cost of falling back to the loopback transport.
IPC_MODE=host
PRIVILEGED=false
#   ULIMIT_NOFILE: DDS opens a socket pair per participant per topic; the stock
#   1024 runs out on a mid-sized graph.
ULIMIT_NOFILE=65535

# [ROS 2 Specific]
#   ROS_DOMAIN_ID: Prevents network clashes in multi-project or multi-team collaboration (0~101)
#   RMW_IMPLEMENTATION: ROS 2 Middleware (Default: CycloneDDS for better stability instead of FastDDS)
# ROS_DOMAIN_ID=0
# RMW_IMPLEMENTATION=rmw_cyclonedds_cpp

# [ROS 1 Specific]
#   ROS_MASTER_URI: ROS 1 Master (roscore) Address
#   ROS_HOSTNAME: Network identifier name of the container
#   ROS_IP: Host IP needed for communication with external devices (uncomment if necessary)
# ROS_MASTER_URI=http://localhost:11311
# ROS_HOSTNAME=localhost
# ROS_IP=127.0.0.1

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 4. HYBRID WORKSPACE (Volumes & Cache)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# [Clean Workspace Strategy]
# Empty value (Default): Named Volume — Build artifacts stay fully isolated inside Docker.
#   → Maintains a clean host directory and is best for data isolation between containers.
# Specific path: Bind Mount — Directly connects to a folder on the host.
#   → Use when you need IDE code completion or direct file access from the host.
#   → Example: ROS_BUILD_VOL=./build
ROS_BUILD_VOL=
ROS_INSTALL_VOL=
ROS_LOG_VOL=
DEV_BUILD_VOL=
DEV_INSTALL_VOL=
DEV_LOG_VOL=

# [Third-party Dependencies]
#   Relative path inside the workspace to clone the dependency sources to (Default: src/thirdparty)
# SYNC_TARGET_DIR=src/thirdparty
#   Fail-open switches for exceptional dependency sync situations.
#   Default behavior fails fast on vcs/rosdep errors to avoid partial environments.
# DEVKIT_VCS_ALLOW_FAILURE=0
# DEVKIT_ROSDEP_ALLOW_FAILURE=0

# [Cache & Performance]
#   Directory for Docker build/runtime caches (ccache, uv, apt, etc.)
#   Default: .docker_cache within the current project root.
#   If set, this must be an absolute, non-root path.
#   DevKit refuses relative paths and the workspace root; shared
#   cache paths are allowed but will be warned before deletion.
# DOCKER_DEV_CACHE_DIR=/home/user/.global_docker_cache

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 5. SYSTEM & BUILD TOOLS
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# APT_SNAPSHOT_DATE: pin Ubuntu APT packages to a date, e.g. 20260714T030000Z
#   (date -u +%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ). The default 'latest' tracks the rolling archive
#   and is NOT reproducible — pin it on release branches.
#   SCOPE: the Ubuntu archive only. ROS/CUDA repos, uv, the Python toolchain,
#   rosdep and fonts are still fetched live (see docs/DEPLOY.md).
#   Example: APT_SNAPSHOT_DATE=20260714T030000Z
APT_SNAPSHOT_DATE=latest

# ROS_SNAPSHOT_DATE: pin the ROS apt layer to snapshots.ros.org.
#   latest (default) | YYYY-MM-DD | final  (final = the distro's EOL snapshot)
#   Use a published snapshot date; snapshots use a separate signing key.
ROS_SNAPSHOT_DATE=latest

#   LANG: Supports Unicode (e.g., Korean/Emoji) while keeping system messages in English.
LANG=C.UTF-8
#   TZ: System timezone (Default: UTC)
TZ=UTC
#   DEBIAN_FRONTEND: Prevents interactive prompts during package installation (Strictly non-interactive)
DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive

# [Option] If system messages must respond in Korean (Not recommended, change before building)
# LANG=ko_KR.UTF-8

# [Build Specs] - Passed to compose and the in-container build helpers as-is.
#   CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD: Passed as -DCMAKE_CXX_STANDARD in colcon build (Supported: 17, 20)
#   CMAKE_C_STANDARD: Passed as -DCMAKE_C_STANDARD (Supported: 11, 17)
CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD=17
CMAKE_C_STANDARD=11

# [Python Specs] - UV_PYTHON is derived from ROS_DISTRO by scripts/check_env.sh; the rest are fixed.
#   SYS_PYTHON_EXE: Path to system python executable in Container
#   UV_VERSION: uv version to use in Container
#   UV_PYTHON: Python version for uv (in Container). Commented out on purpose —
#   ROS_DISTRO decides it (20.04/noetic,foxy: 3.8 | 22.04/humble,iron: 3.10 |
#   24.04/jazzy,kilted,rolling: 3.12), because apt puts rclpy and rospy in the
#   system interpreter. Set it only to build against a pairing DevKit does not know.
SYS_PYTHON_EXE=/usr/bin/python3
UV_VERSION=0.10.10
# UV_PYTHON=3.10

# [Build Flags] - Manage dependencies and build options per project.
#   UV_EXTRA: which [project.optional-dependencies] extra `uvs` installs
#   (e.g. cpu | gpu, once your pyproject declares them — the template ships a
#   commented example). Describes the TARGET, not the machine you build on —
#   a GPU-less CI runner still builds a gpu image when this says gpu.
UV_EXTRA=
#   Python dependency synchronization flags (e.g., --extra gpu, --extra cpu)
UV_SYNC_FLAGS=
#   Extra CMake arguments (e.g., -DUSE_CUDA=ON)
CMAKE_EXTRA_ARGS=
#   Extra ROS colcon build arguments (e.g., --executor sequential)
COLCON_EXTRA_FLAGS=

# [Security] GPG key verification policy during image build (ROS + NVIDIA CUDA).
#   On a pinned-fingerprint mismatch (key rotation OR tampering):
#     true (default) = abort the build (fail-closed; blocks key substitution)
#     false          = warn but continue (fail-open; opt-in for iteration speed)
#   NOTE: apt still verifies package signatures against the key in BOTH cases;
#   this pin only detects whether the key itself was swapped. On a real ROS
#   rotation, run 'make update-gpg' to refresh the pinned fingerprint.
# STRICT_GPG_CHECK=true

# [TERMINAL]: Terminal emulator launched by `make term ENV=ros|dev`
#   Must be installed inside the dev container image. Nothing is by default:
#   uncomment 'terminator # gui' in dependencies/apt.txt (or name another).
# TERMINAL=terminator

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 6. PRODUCTION & RUNTIME (Production Only)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

#   IMAGE_TAG: Tag to use for production image building (Default: latest)
#   Must be a valid Docker tag: start with alnum/_; use alnum, _, ., -; max 128 chars.
IMAGE_TAG=latest
#   SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH: Optional Unix timestamp for reproducible release metadata.
#   Example (a shell command, so pass it on the command line, not in this file):
#     SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH=$(git log -1 --format=%ct) make bake-prod
# SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH=

#   SIF_MODE: Select run target for `make run-sif` (dev | prod | slurm).
#   ENV selects the prod SIF family when SIF_MODE=prod|slurm.
# SIF_MODE=dev
#   SIF_FILE: Override generated SIF filename when needed.
# SIF_FILE=/scratch/user/images/myproject-ros-prod-amd64.sif
#   BAKE_FORMAT: sif (default) or oci. `oci` stops at a docker-archive tarball,
#   which is what you want on macOS — convert it to a SIF on a Linux host.
# BAKE_FORMAT=sif

#   [PROD] Production image run command (Edit strictly to your App requirements).
#   Precedence: RUN_ARGS on the make line > ROS_LAUNCH_COMMAND > APP_COMMAND.
#   Production/slurm runs fail fast unless one of them is set.
#   (Example)
# ROS_LAUNCH_COMMAND=ros2 launch bringup main.launch.py
# APP_COMMAND=python3 main.py

#   These four are read by the scripts but belong to no #SBATCH default, so they
#   are declared here too — `make check` compares .env against this file, and an
#   undeclared knob is invisible to it.
#   DEVKIT_SLURM_EXTRA_ARGS: extra sbatch flags, passed verbatim (docs/SLURM.md)
# DEVKIT_SLURM_EXTRA_ARGS=
#   DEVKIT_VERIFY_SIF_HASH: 1 re-hashes the SIF when opening a run record
# DEVKIT_VERIFY_SIF_HASH=0
#   DEVKIT_REPO_ROOT: where the COMPUTE nodes see this workspace, when it differs
# DEVKIT_REPO_ROOT=
#   APT_SNAPSHOT_FALLBACK: 1 lets a pinned build fall back to rolling mirrors
#   when snapshot.ubuntu.com is unreachable (the artifact records that it did)
# APT_SNAPSHOT_FALLBACK=false
#   DEVKIT_REQUIRE_PINNED: 1 refuses a .repos entry that is not a full commit
#   hash (prod bakes set it themselves)
# DEVKIT_REQUIRE_PINNED=
#   DEVKIT_ALLOW_EXTERNAL_SYNC_TARGET: 1 lets `sync_deps --force` reset a target
#   outside the workspace (never the workspace itself)
# DEVKIT_ALLOW_EXTERNAL_SYNC_TARGET=
#   DEVKIT_STRIP_SOURCE / DEVKIT_FAIL_ON_SOURCE: build-stage only — byte-compile
#   and delete .py modules in the artifact / refuse to ship plaintext source.
#   Pass them to `make bake-prod`, not from here: they DELETE files.
#   DEVKIT_SKIP_CLANG_FORMAT: 1 accepts an unchecked C/C++ half in `make lint`
# DEVKIT_SKIP_CLANG_FORMAT=

#   [SLURM] Uncomment to override scripts/slurm_run.sh's #SBATCH defaults.
#   Resource defaults below must match the header (checked by [slurm-defaults]).
# DEVKIT_SLURM_JOB_NAME=devkit
# DEVKIT_SLURM_NODES=1
# DEVKIT_SLURM_NTASKS=1
# DEVKIT_SLURM_CPUS_PER_TASK=8
# DEVKIT_SLURM_MEM=32G
# DEVKIT_SLURM_TIME=02:00:00
# DEVKIT_SLURM_OUTPUT=logs/%x_%j.out
# DEVKIT_SLURM_ERROR=logs/%x_%j.err
# DEVKIT_SLURM_SIGNAL=B:TERM@60
#   No default — these depend on the site, and a wrong guess is worse than none.
#   Without GRES no GPU is allocated, whatever the image can do.
# DEVKIT_SLURM_PARTITION=gpu
# DEVKIT_SLURM_GRES=gpu:1
# DEVKIT_SLURM_ACCOUNT=my-allocation
# DEVKIT_SLURM_COMMENT=submitter:devkit
# DEVKIT_SLURM_ARRAY=0-9%4
# SLURM_DATA_ROOT=/path/to/read-only/data
# SLURM_RUN_ROOT=/path/to/writeable/runs
# CONTAINER_DATA_ROOT=/workspace/data
# CONTAINER_RUN_ROOT=/runs

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 7. HOST INTEGRATION (Git & SSH Agent)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# [SSH Authentication]
#   This project uses the host's SSH Agent for secure authentication inside the container.
#   No manual path configuration is required as long as `ssh-agent` is running on the host.
#   To add your keys, run: `ssh-add ~/.ssh/id_rsa` (on the host) before `make start ENV=ros`.

# [Git Configuration]
#   Shares the host's configuration (Read-Only) for Git Identity (Commit/Push) inside the container.
#   It automatically reads standard file locations (~/.gitconfig) from the host.
#   Default value: ${HOST_HOME}/.gitconfig
#   If you want to use a specific location, uncomment below and set an existing file path.
# GIT_CONFIG_PATH=/home/me/.gitconfig

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 8. LOGGING & DIAGNOSTICS
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

#   LOG_SHOW_TIME: Show timestamps on the CONSOLE (true | false). A LOG_FILE
#                  always carries a date and time, whatever this says.
LOG_SHOW_TIME=false

#   DEBUG_MODE: Enable verbose debug log (log_debug) outputs (true | false)
DEBUG_MODE=false

#   LOG_FILE: Also write every log line to a file, without colour (relative to
#             WORKSPACE_PATH unless absolute). Set to 'off' to disable it —
#             including the container boot log the entrypoint keeps by default.
#   Example: log/setup.log will be stored in ${WORKSPACE_PATH}/log/setup.log.
# LOG_FILE=log/setup.log
